# Build-time environment for the Vite client bundle.
#
#   cp .env.build.example .env.build   # then fill in the two values
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM .env.production
#
# Vite does not read configuration at runtime. It substitutes
# import.meta.env.VITE_FOO with the literal string while it compiles, so these
# two values are frozen into dist/client/assets/*.js at build time. Compose's
# `env_file:` only sets the container's process environment after that build is
# finished — it cannot reach back into an already-compiled bundle.
#
# Build args are also NOT read from env_file:. Compose resolves `${VAR}` in
# build.args from the shell environment or from the --env-file it was invoked
# with, which is why the deploy command is:
#
#   docker compose --env-file .env.build -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --build
#
# OMITTING THIS FILE PRODUCES A BLANK WHITE PAGE, NOT A BUILD ERROR. The bundle
# still builds and still loads; it throws "Missing Supabase browser
# configuration" during React's first render, nothing is ever committed to
# #root, and the site serves an empty page. The Dockerfile now fails the build
# if these args are missing, and deploy.sh asserts the built bundle contains the
# URL, specifically so this cannot fail silently again.
#
# ONLY PUBLIC VALUES GO IN THIS FILE. Everything here ends up readable in the
# shipped JavaScript. VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY is designed to be public
# (it is sent to every browser and is constrained by RLS), so it is safe here.
# NEVER add SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, or any other secret to
# this file, and never rename a secret to a VITE_* name — Vite would inline it
# into the client bundle and publish it to the world.
# See docs/JOOBLE_INTEGRATION.md §3.4.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
